WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Speaker 1: So there's a new study which finds&nbsp;
that legalizing cannabis, also known as marijuana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:16.240
pot, weed, et cetera, legalizing cannabis lowers&nbsp;
demand for prescription drugs. And this really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.880
shouldn't come as a surprise to us. I mean,&nbsp;
if we want to focus in just narrowly on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:30.240
we know that sometimes cannabis can be a&nbsp;
substitute for pharmaceutical anxiety medication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:37.120
We can know that cannabis. We know that cannabis&nbsp;
is sometimes a workable substitute for Big Pharma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:44.320
sleep medication. We know that cannabis can be a&nbsp;
replacement for opiate pain medication. There's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:52.560
long list, and it's not the idea that cannabis is&nbsp;
this panacea cure all substance, but we know from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:00.640
good studies that in many cases, you can find&nbsp;
a cannabis based alternative for a variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.640
different symptoms where you might reach for some&nbsp;
other kind of medication. Now, whether we say it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.520
better to reach for the cannabis or not, I'm&nbsp;
not even evaluating that in this segment. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.560
actually doesn't matter for our purposes. But&nbsp;
the point is we know that there is this very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.800
the tentacles of opposition to cannabis&nbsp;
legalization come from a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.600
areas. And now we have a new study which points&nbsp;
very specifically to this aspect of it. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.280
a good piece in the hill about this. Legalizing&nbsp;
marijuana lowers demand for prescription drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:43.440
study finds the reductions in drug utilization&nbsp;
that we find could lead could lead to significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.480
cost savings for state Medicaid programs. The&nbsp;
results also indicate an opportunity to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.840
the harm that can come with the dangerous side&nbsp;
effects associated with some prescription drugs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:00.000
One researcher said I encourage you to read about&nbsp;
the study and the details about the study. But as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.680
I pointed out, they found a decline in the volume&nbsp;
of prescriptions targeting pain, depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:13.840
anxiety, sleep, psychosis and seizures in states&nbsp;
that have legalized recreational use. So what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.560
the kind of important takeaways as we think about&nbsp;
this as a political issue? It makes a lot of sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:27.200
that lots of different elements would want to keep&nbsp;
cannabis illegal, or at least not recreationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:34.160
legal. It increases profits in a number of&nbsp;
different areas, I'm only going to mention a few,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:40.640
there's probably more so. Big Pharma. If you have&nbsp;
widespread recreational cannabis legalization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.880
Big Pharma profits would likely go down in those&nbsp;
places. Private prisons, we've said before, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.000
know, private prisons are not as big in the United&nbsp;
States as some people think. I think the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:03:02.320
study I saw says private prison inmates are nine&nbsp;
or 11 percent of federal inmates and like zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.080
percent or close to zero at the state level. I&nbsp;
believe that that's true. But to some degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.760
when you have more reasons why someone&nbsp;
might be imprisoned, that's good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.000
private prison profits, for sure. The bail&nbsp;
bonds industry, if you take something that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:25.680
currently against the law and you make it legal,&nbsp;
fewer people will demand the service of bail bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.960
lawyers, right? I mean, to some&nbsp;
degree, if you eliminate a crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.160
you will eliminate the need for lawyers to&nbsp;
defend people against charges for that crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.760
We also know that cannabis legalization lowers&nbsp;
demand for alcohol. I don't know, but I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.480
if it reduces the demand for cigarets. Maybe&nbsp;
I don't know enough about the cigaret piece to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.160
be able to say for sure, and I'm sure that I'm&nbsp;
missing many here in my discussion of this. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:00.720
this is both why, on the one hand, a lot of these&nbsp;
different industries would want to keep cannabis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:08.640
illegal. Oh, sorry, I forgot a major one. Police&nbsp;
departments seizures, both of drugs, but also of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.720
cash associated with the dealing of cannabis, etc.&nbsp;
That's also another big aspect of this. Police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.160
departments, local police departments receiving&nbsp;
significant moneys as a result of cannabis not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.560
being recreationally legal or even decriminalized&nbsp;
at all. So on the one hand, that's why Big Pharma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.480
would oppose cannabis legalization. But at&nbsp;
the same time, it seems Big Pharma realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.080
the writing is sort of on the wall, and Big&nbsp;
Pharma has also been pursuing patents related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.520
cannabis. So it's like they don't like it until&nbsp;
they take over the business and can monetize it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.360
at which point they really like it. None of this&nbsp;
should surprise us. And if you've been following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.080
the political discussion around cannabis for&nbsp;
any period of time or for more than a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:05:02.720
as we have on the show, you would understand the&nbsp;
incongruity and we could really call it a double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.120
standard or hypocrisy that exists compared to&nbsp;
so many different things. When you look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.960
Schedule one federal designation of cannabis&nbsp;
up there with the most damaging and addicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.120
substances of no medicinal value, when hold&nbsp;
on a second, we we know I mean the study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:26.320
Big Pharma concedes that legal cannabis is&nbsp;
a threat to their profits because it is of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.720
medicinal value without calling it a panacea. Of&nbsp;
course, you know that the Schedule one designation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.240
doesn't make any sense. And we've talked about&nbsp;
all of these aspects before. So here is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:45.280
one. And I still on this. You know, there was&nbsp;
a period I remember early in the show's history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:53.200
where we started to see gay marriage legalization&nbsp;
in one state, in another state, in a third, state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.800
in a fourth state. And at the time, I remember&nbsp;
conversations with the team and with guests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.840
There was a certain feeling of inevitability&nbsp;
that it's hard to imagine 10 years down the line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.840
that gay couples lesbian couples wouldn't be able&nbsp;
to get married anywhere in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.960
And indeed, ultimately it happened. I have&nbsp;
a similar feeling of inevitability to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.920
degree when it comes to cannabis. I don't know how&nbsp;
soon we're going to see recreational legalization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.280
nationally. I don't know about that, but there's a&nbsp;
sense of inevitability that we are heading in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.800
direction, at least of federal decriminalization.&nbsp;
I don't know when it will come. But there's that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.880
same sort of sense that I have. If you disagree,&nbsp;
let me know I'd be interested in hearing from you.

